{
  "paraId": "51AF64F4",
  "style": "",
  "text": "The optimal doses of antiangiogenic agents contribute to improving TIME 27. A previous study shows that the addition of anlotinib (3 mg/kg) boosts the antitumor efficacy of radioimmunotherapy 28. However, it does not explore the effect of different doses of anlotinib on TIME. In the current study, we found that anlotinib (3 mg/kg) combined with radiation did not increase the percentage of CD8+ T cells compared to IR alone (Supplementary Fig. 8). It is reported that anlotinib (1.5 mg/kg) could break through the immunosuppressive barrier to potentiate TIME 29. Consistently, we found that low-dose (1.5 mg/kg) anlotinib combined with IR could normalize blood vessels (Supplementary Fig. 9). Hence, low-dose (1.5 mg/kg) anlotinib was selected for subsequent experiments. However, the optimal dose for anlotinib is yet controversial and requires further study."
}

